WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
Speaker 1: Let's start today talking about&nbsp;
food shortages, there is a new storyline, a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.920
narrative claims that food shortages are imminent&nbsp;
in the United States and that there is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:17.920
no food. The shelves will be bare as the failed&nbsp;
former President Donald Trump would often like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:24.240
to say Joe Biden even said it. This was about&nbsp;
three weeks ago, and the context was the Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.280
invasion of Ukraine and the related sanctions&nbsp;
and responses. Here's Joe Biden three weeks ago,&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.960
Speaker 2: and nothing more to report with&nbsp;
regard to food shortages. Yes, we did.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:40.720
So talk about food shortages and and it's&nbsp;
going to be real. The price of these sanctions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:47.040
has not just imposed upon Russia, it's imposed&nbsp;
upon an awful lot of countries as well, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.920
European countries in our country as well.
Speaker 1: So on the other hand, we also have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:59.040
kind of the more hyperbolic, fear based kind of&nbsp;
reactionary claims. Here's one example from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:04.160
right wing host, Dan Bongino, who had a segment&nbsp;
called The Devastating Food Crisis is coming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:10.320
Please prepare now and the preppers loving this.
Speaker 3: The food crisis coming up. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.120
topic we've covered on the show and the radio&nbsp;
show often, right? There is a devastating food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.280
crisis coming. Ladies and gentlemen, and you darn&nbsp;
well, better be prepared. Listen, I care about you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.440
a lot. I'm not kidding. I love the&nbsp;
audience. It's why there's an email&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:27.120
on our website. That's why I leave the&nbsp;
Facebook page open for your messages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:32.189
right? I am here for you. I am one of you. I came&nbsp;
from you, a middle class kid who had a middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.189 --> 00:01:36.360
class job with a middle class life.
Speaker 1: OK, so anyway, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.480
without his full biography, the point is the&nbsp;
right is increasingly saying food shortages are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:48.080
coming. Many of the people predicting the food&nbsp;
shortages will also sell you food packaged food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.960
bonus buckets. There's a lot of comments if you&nbsp;
look at the Dan Bongino piece where people saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:59.920
from my Bible study group we can get food and all&nbsp;
of this different stuff. So let's talk it through.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.960
Is it possible that we could have a food shortage?&nbsp;
Of course. Of course, there have been food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:10.160
shortages in history and they are possible.&nbsp;
The first step in really thinking critically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.720
about this is what do we mean by a food shortage?&nbsp;
Because there are some people who seem to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:22.000
that if food shortages? My favorite orange juice&nbsp;
wasn't available, I had to get pulp instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:28.640
no pulp. Or I like turkey bacon, but there was no&nbsp;
turkey bacon, so I had to consider regular bacon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:34.480
or plant based bacon. That's not really a food&nbsp;
shortage. Now I'm going to get to that, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.720
is the products we expect just aren't there. OK,&nbsp;
we're going to talk about that in a moment, but a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:45.120
food shortage when we talk about it economically&nbsp;
is there is not enough energy or there's close to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.640
not enough energy, meaning calories available&nbsp;
for the proper nutrition of a population.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.800
And I would even go further. I would say there's&nbsp;
not enough calories available from a reasonably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:03.200
diverse set of foods. What I mean by that is&nbsp;
imagine that in order to meet our caloric needs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.920
everybody had to be drinking corn sirup like,&nbsp;
OK, we'd be getting our calories. But I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.400
still consider that to be a food shortage because&nbsp;
it's not a calorie shortage, but it's definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:18.160
a shortage of a reasonably diverse diet. So&nbsp;
the first question is, is an actual shortage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.720
based on that softer definition, not even going&nbsp;
to be able to really get the food groups we need.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:29.760
Is that likely this year based on the evidence we&nbsp;
have? And the answer is no. It's hard to say more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:35.280
but there's not going to be a food group calorie&nbsp;
deficit. There is going to be enough meat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:39.840
there's going to be enough dairy, there's going&nbsp;
to be enough produce, there will be enough grains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:46.800
We're not going to actually have a shortage&nbsp;
of calories from any particular food group.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:54.000
Now, might there be a situation where&nbsp;
in Portland, Oregon? There's no chicken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.000
breast for a day at the local store&nbsp;
and you've got to get chicken thigh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.440
Yeah, that that could happen, though those&nbsp;
things anecdotally are happening and we'll talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:09.600
about why in a moment. But my store was out of a&nbsp;
particular cookie, and so I had to get the Milano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:15.200
you know, I mean, most of us aren't savages, so we&nbsp;
get dark chocolate. You might have to get the milk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.760
chocolate. I know it's not good. It's ugly, I get&nbsp;
it. It's ugly. I, you know, I'm for dark chocolate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.720
as much as the next guy. But that's not really&nbsp;
a food shortage, right? The shelves aren't bare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.040
Now let's get into what might happen. Just so we&nbsp;
all know and when we're aware and we're prepared.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.960
Is it possible that specific products might ebb&nbsp;
and flow in their supply so that you might have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.640
to choose something different that you're&nbsp;
used to getting? Yeah, that is possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.360
and that's definitely different than what most of&nbsp;
us in the United States are used to. The war in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:51.760
Ukraine is likely to disrupt wheat and sunflower&nbsp;
seed oil and sunflower seed oil is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:57.120
in a lot of foods that many of us buy. But it's&nbsp;
more likely to be disruptive to countries other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.480
than the United States. The U.S. is actually a big&nbsp;
wheat exporter and producer. Poorer nations that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:08.000
demand that rely more heavily on on imports of&nbsp;
wheat are going to be more at risk, for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:14.000
So like the wheat issue will hit Egypt, Indonesia,&nbsp;
Bangladesh, Pakistan, Turkey, some other countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:20.240
there will likely not be shortages, but there&nbsp;
might be some limited availability of meat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:27.840
dairy and some animal products. There might&nbsp;
also be a shortage of lack of availability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:33.200
I think is the word I should say of some plant&nbsp;
based vegan proteins those for two reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:39.960
One. There are some people who if they don't see&nbsp;
the meat that they want, they might try the plant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:45.360
based alternative. The plant based alternatives&nbsp;
are also highly processed foods, and the supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:50.720
chain can't be accelerated that easily. A lot of&nbsp;
those processed items that you can't easily adjust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:55.840
the supply chain quickly. It can take six to nine&nbsp;
months, so you might see that. So we might see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:02.160
slight, sporadic regional temporary lack of&nbsp;
availability of certain products. Now what could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:09.440
cause the appearance of an actual shortage? Panic&nbsp;
buying like we saw early in the pandemic? And in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:15.680
that case, you know, is it really a shortage if&nbsp;
a ton of people are pre purchasing a year's worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.320
of whatever? And what I mean by that is think&nbsp;
back to the so-called toilet paper shortage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.000
There was no toilet paper on the shelves. That's&nbsp;
true. But the reason was that a ton of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:33.840
bought a year's supply, so they're good now for a&nbsp;
year. And as we saw, once the supply normalized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:37.920
we had fewer people buying the toilet paper&nbsp;
because they pre-purchase it in the first month&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.800
of the pandemic. So that was an apparent shortage.&nbsp;
It wasn't a real shortage. It's also, by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:48.000
interesting how a lot of the producers of some of&nbsp;
these foods Tyson Perdue, for example, in poultry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.440
they've had record profits above and beyond the&nbsp;
rate of inflation. So it's not just inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:59.840
that's driving their profit at a time of allegedly&nbsp;
significantly higher costs to them. So I'm not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:07.040
ready to say that there is price gouging going on,&nbsp;
but it is interesting that profit margins remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:12.160
at record highs despite supposedly higher costs&nbsp;
and inflation and all of that. So we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:16.960
to follow it. That's the best assessment we have&nbsp;
right now based on the information that we have

